WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:08.080
Speaker 1: There is a very rapidly growing in&nbsp;
concern movement among the Republican Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.720
about how to get control of the party&nbsp;
back in advance of the 22 midterms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:20.160
certainly in advance of the 2024 Republican&nbsp;
presidential primary. Now you might be saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.760
well, get control back from who, or you might&nbsp;
understand that the question is about getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.760
control back from Donald Trump, and there are&nbsp;
a number of interesting articles about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.920
that have come out over the last 24 hours.&nbsp;
One such article in The Washington Post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:41.280
reports about clashes between Republican leaders&nbsp;
and the candidates that Trump has embraced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.040
and that these clashes have been playing off&nbsp;
playing out around the country. A chaotic sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.560
that Trump's once unchallenged hold on the party&nbsp;
and rank and file supporters is waning even by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.200
degrees. And I think at this point, we have to&nbsp;
say Trump's control over the party is waning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.240
but only to a degree. In other words, we see&nbsp;
that the number of Republicans, the percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.240
of Republicans that want Trump to run again&nbsp;
is lower, but it's still not low. It's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.560
above 50 percent of Republicans. At last check.&nbsp;
But interestingly, the Washington Post writes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.320
the former president's power within the party&nbsp;
and his continued focus on personal grievances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.040
is increasingly being questioned behind closed&nbsp;
doors at Republican gatherings, according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.840
interviews with more than a dozen prominent&nbsp;
Republicans in Washington and across the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.040
including some Trump advisers. Not surprisingly,&nbsp;
many spoke on the condition of anonymity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.880
because there remains significant fear of&nbsp;
attracting Trump's public wrath, also being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.840
reported by Business Insider on the basis of The&nbsp;
Washington Post report. Republican leaders are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:50.240
holding closed door meetings to question Trump's&nbsp;
power over, the party, report says. Now, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:57.920
it's an important reminder, too, to tell you guys.&nbsp;
Questioning Trump's power is very different than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:04.720
questioning Trump's questioning Trump ism, or the&nbsp;
sweeping authoritarian slide that the Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.760
Party experienced under Trump. But we'll get&nbsp;
to that in a moment. Discontent with Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.200
is growing among some Republican leaders. Senior&nbsp;
Republicans are questioning Trump's hold over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:21.200
